A viable family emerged from Section 3’s native objects. Proposition 3.4 reduces factorization on a line to recovering a degree-\(n\) polynomial from \(n+1\) evaluations. I’m using a hidden arithmetic lattice so the planted factors compose by a rising-factorial identity: generic exact interpolation plus bounded rational-root scanning costs hundreds of thousands to millions of field operations, while recognizing the lattice reduces recovery to two adjacent coordinates and a short exact calculation. This is an explicit Track B claim, not an average-case hardness claim.
